  <!-- UserGuide: Page Header -->
  <data name="UserGuide_PageHeaderTitle.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>USER GUIDE</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_PageHeaderDescription.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Everything you need to know to get the most out of Agent-X.</value></data>

  <!-- UserGuide: Welcome (Row 1) -->
  <data name="UserGuide_Welcome_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Welcome to Agent-X</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Welcome_Description1.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Agent-X is a local-first AI workspace for thinking, researching and writing with your own documents. By default it chats with a built-in model that runs on your computer, and it keeps your documents and conversations in a local SQLite database, which you can encrypt from Settings. Nothing leaves your computer unless you choose a cloud AI provider, set up web search or a calendar or email connector, or import pages from the web; the bottom of the Dashboard lists the services that are switched on.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Welcome_Description2.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>In one app you can chat with the built-in model, with models you run in Ollama, or with OpenAI and Anthropic models once you add an API key; ask questions about your own documents and get answers drawn from them (Retrieval-Augmented Generation, RAG); build a Knowledge Vault with automatic tagging, duplicate detection and a Knowledge Graph; search by meaning, by keyword or both (hybrid search merges FTS5 keyword matches and semantic results with Reciprocal Rank Fusion); bring in web pages with the browser extension or Web Import; transcribe audio files; run multi-step Workflows; and extend Agent-X with plugins, calendar and email connectors, and an Android companion app that browses and searches your vault.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Welcome_Description3.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>This guide walks through the pages of Agent-X in one scrolling column. To get somewhere fast from anywhere in the app, press Ctrl+K for the Command Palette (pages and a few actions) or Ctrl+P for Jump To (pages, documents and conversations).</value></data>

  <!-- UserGuide: Getting Started (Row 2) -->
  <data name="UserGuide_GettingStarted_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Getting Started</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_GettingStarted_Prerequisites_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Prerequisites</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_GettingStarted_Prereq1.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Install Ollama (optional) - Agent-X uses its built-in local model by default, so you need Ollama only if you want to run other open models on your computer. Download it from ollama.com. OpenAI and Anthropic need only an API key.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_GettingStarted_Prereq2_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Pull at least one chat model — Open a terminal and run</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_GettingStarted_Prereq2_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>llama3.2 is a good general-purpose chat model and runs well with 8 GB of VRAM. Indexing, Semantic Search and Ask Your Files also need embeddings: with the default Embedding Model setting (all-minilm), Agent-X uses its built-in model when that model is installed and otherwise asks Ollama for all-minilm, a small embedding model (about 46 MB) that you pull with:</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_GettingStarted_Prereq3_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Keep Ollama running - When Agent-X uses Ollama, Ollama must be serving on http://localhost:11434 (the default address). If it is not already running in the background, start it with</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_GettingStarted_FirstLaunch_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>First Launch &amp; Onboarding</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_GettingStarted_FirstLaunch_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>The first time you open Agent-X the Onboarding wizard takes over. It tests the connection to your Ollama endpoint (you can skip this step), lists your locally installed Ollama models so you can pick a default chat model and a default embedding model, checks the built-in local model and your GPU acceleration (and offers to download the model if your installer did not include it), and takes optional OpenAI and Anthropic API keys before a final summary. You import documents afterwards, from the Knowledge Vault.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_GettingStarted_FirstLaunch_Tip.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>The Dashboard's Setup AI button opens Settings, where you can change the AI provider, models, and API keys at any time. To walk through the wizard again, press Ctrl+P and choose Onboarding. Leaving the wizard before its final Launch Agent-X step (with a shortcut, the command palette, or Jump To) counts as skipping it, so it does not come back on the next launch.</value></data>

  <!-- UserGuide: Dashboard (Row 3) -->
  <data name="UserGuide_Dashboard_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Dashboard</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Dashboard_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>The Dashboard is the page Agent-X opens on at startup, and Ctrl+D brings you back to it. On one scrolling page it shows the state of your AI provider, your Knowledge Vault and your recent work, with buttons to the pages behind each figure.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Dashboard_Features_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>What you will find on the Dashboard:</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Dashboard_Feature1.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>AI Status and Quick Search - The top card names the active AI provider (the built-in model by default), whether it is reachable, and the current model; Setup AI opens Settings. Type in the search box below it and press Enter to open Semantic Search with your query.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Dashboard_Feature2.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Vault and System Figures - Four cards count your documents and Collections, the storage your documents use and the share of them already indexed, your conversations and the tokens used, and your GPU, RAM and video memory. File Type Distribution and Top Collections break the vault down further, and the strip at the bottom shows available RAM, vault storage, and whether any service is set to send data off your computer.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Dashboard_Feature3.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Recent Work and Operations - Recent Documents and Recent Conversations list your latest imports and chats; View All opens the Knowledge Vault or AI Chat. The Operations Overview sums up conversation intelligence, sync health, connectors and plugins, the Smart Inbox backlog and workflow activity, each with a button to its page.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Dashboard_Feature4.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Belief Evolution and Next Steps - Your Belief Evolution lists up to five topics whose recorded stance has changed, with the earlier and the current stance and the size of the change; acknowledge one to dismiss it for good, or open Past Self. Recommended Next Steps suggests what to do next, such as finishing AI setup, indexing waiting documents, triaging the Smart Inbox or creating a workflow.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Dashboard_QuickActions_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Quick-Action Buttons</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Dashboard_QuickActions_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>The Setup AI button at the top and the row of tiles at the bottom of the Dashboard (New Chat, Import Files, Search, Ask Files and Quick Actions) cover the most common tasks:</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Dashboard_QuickAction1_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Setup AI</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Dashboard_QuickAction1_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Open Settings to reconfigure the AI provider, Ollama, and models.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Dashboard_QuickAction2_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Import Documents</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Dashboard_QuickAction2_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Add files to your Knowledge Vault for AI-powered analysis.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Dashboard_QuickAction3_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Start Chat</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Dashboard_QuickAction3_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Open AI Chat with the active model. It returns to the conversation you had open, so press Ctrl+N for a new one.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Dashboard_QuickAction4_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Semantic Search</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Dashboard_QuickAction4_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Search your documents by meaning, not just keywords.</value></data>

  <!-- UserGuide: AI Chat (Row 4) -->
  <data name="UserGuide_AiChat_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>AI Chat</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_AiChat_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>AI Chat is where you talk to a model. It uses the provider chosen in Settings: the built-in local model (the default), a model you run in Ollama, or OpenAI or Anthropic once you add an API key. Replies stream in as they are written and render basic Markdown (headings, lists, bold, inline code, and code blocks with syntax highlighting), and every conversation is saved in the local database.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_AiChat_Using_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Using AI Chat</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_AiChat_Step1.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Select a model - The model box at the top of the chat lists the active provider's models by name; pick one to switch to it. To use another provider, change it in Settings. If the provider was not reachable, the Refresh connection button in the top bar checks again and reloads the list.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_AiChat_Step2.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Type your message - Write in the box at the bottom. Enter sends the message and Shift+Enter starts a new line. The microphone button records speech and puts the transcript in the box so you can check it before sending (see Audio Transcription).</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_AiChat_Step3.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Watch the response stream - The reply appears as it is generated; Stop generation ends it early and keeps what was written so far. Each reply has buttons to rate, copy, regenerate or delete it, and your own messages can be copied, edited (Save &amp; Resend sends the new text) or deleted.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_AiChat_Conversation_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Conversation Management</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_AiChat_Conversation1.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Conversations are saved automatically in the local SQLite database (encrypted only if you turned on database encryption in Settings). The sidebar lists pinned conversations first, then the most recent. The search box finds words in titles and message text, and the folder buttons (All plus each folder in use) filter the list.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_AiChat_Conversation2.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Press Ctrl+N anywhere, or Ctrl+Shift+N on the chat page, to start a new conversation, and Ctrl+B to show or hide the conversation list. Each conversation keeps its own history, so the model can refer back to what was said earlier in it.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_AiChat_Conversation3.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Right-click a conversation in the list to pin, unpin or delete it; Agent-X asks before deleting. The folder button in the top bar moves the open conversation to Work, Research, Personal, Archive or a folder you name. Export conversation in the top bar and Export all conversations in the sidebar save conversations to a file (see Export &amp; Data Portability). Conversations cannot be renamed, and there is no command that clears the whole history.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_AiChat_Advanced_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Advanced Chat Features</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_AiChat_Advanced1.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Conversation Branching - Choose Branch from here on one of your own messages to continue from that point in a separate branch; the original thread stays as it was. When a conversation has branches, a Branches list appears below the conversation list: select a branch to open it, or use its buttons to merge it into the main thread (Agent-X asks first) or delete it. Compare branches in the top bar opens the main thread and the first branch side by side in a new window.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_AiChat_Advanced2.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>System Prompts and Code Blocks - The prompt button in the top bar applies one of ten built-in system prompts, such as Code Helper, Summarizer or Socratic Teacher, to the messages you send; a new conversation starts without one, and you cannot add your own prompts yet. Code blocks in replies show their language, syntax highlighting and a Copy button.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_AiChat_Advanced3.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Memory and Context - After each reply, Agent-X asks the model in the background to note facts worth keeping (preferences, project details, instructions) and stores them on your computer. When you send a message, up to eight stored facts that closely match it are added to the model's context, whichever conversation they came from; the app cannot list or delete them yet. With a cloud provider, this is one more billed call per reply. Inspect Context shows what was assembled for a reply, including the conversation's Durable Summary (which you can refresh there) and messages recalled from earlier conversations.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_AiChat_Advanced4.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Suggested Follow-Ups and Token Counts - After each reply, Agent-X asks the model for up to three follow-up questions and shows them as chips; clicking one puts it in the message box. That is an extra model call, which cloud providers bill like any other. A counter in the chat header adds up the tokens of the replies you generate, and the Cost Tracking card in Settings estimates OpenAI and Anthropic spend (total and today) from the app's built-in price table; local models cost nothing.</value></data>

  <!-- UserGuide: Ask Your Files (Row 5) -->
  <data name="UserGuide_AskYourFiles_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Ask Your Files (RAG)</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_AskYourFiles_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Ask Your Files is the Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) page of Agent-X. Ask a natural-language question and Agent-X retrieves the most relevant passages from your indexed documents, gives them to the active chat model as context, and streams an answer with numbered citations such as [1] that point to the source documents. The model is told to answer only from those passages and to say when they are not enough, but check the cited sources for anything that matters.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_AskYourFiles_HowItWorks_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>How It Works</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_AskYourFiles_Step1.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Choose a scope - Pick a Collection in the list at the top, or leave it on All Collections to search the whole vault. Narrowing the scope helps when the vault is large; you cannot pick individual documents.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_AskYourFiles_Step2.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Ask a question - Type a natural-language question and press Enter. Agent-X asks the model for a few rephrasings of the question and, for questions of 80 characters or more, also drafts a hypothetical answer (HyDE); each version is searched, which helps recall for abstract questions at the cost of extra model calls.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_AskYourFiles_Step3.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Hybrid retrieval - Each version runs a semantic search (cosine similarity over the vector index) and an FTS5 keyword search, merged with Reciprocal Rank Fusion (k=60). The passages are then deduplicated, reranked (a heuristic pass that spreads results across documents, then a model-based pass), widened with neighboring text and compressed, keeping as many as Top-K Results in Settings &gt; Knowledge Vault allows (5 by default).</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_AskYourFiles_Step4.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Grounded answer - The passages go into the prompt as numbered context blocks, and the model is told to cite them by number. The answer streams in; afterwards each [N] that matches a passage becomes a card in the Sources panel, and numbers with no matching passage are ignored.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_AskYourFiles_Citations_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>The Citation System</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_AskYourFiles_Citations_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>The Sources panel lists each cited passage with its number, document name, page number when the format has pages, match percentage and a short excerpt. Open source document shows the file in File Explorer; it does not jump to the cited passage.</value></data>

  <!-- UserGuide: Quick Actions (Row 6) -->
  <data name="UserGuide_QuickActions_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Quick Actions</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_QuickActions_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Quick Actions runs AI and maintenance tasks on your vault from one page. Pick a document from the list (one at a time) to summarize it or extract its key points, translate text you paste in, scan the whole vault for duplicates, or get Collection and tag suggestions for documents that are not in a Collection yet. Results appear on the page; nothing is saved to the document or applied automatically.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_QuickActions_Available_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Available Actions</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_QuickActions_Action1_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Summarize</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_QuickActions_Action1_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Generate Layered Summary summarizes the selected document from its indexed chunks: the sections are summarized first, then combined into a final overview, so long documents are covered without one oversized prompt. The document must be indexed.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_QuickActions_Action2_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Extract Key Points</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_QuickActions_Action2_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Pulls the main facts, figures and takeaways of the selected document into a bullet list, from the same layered pass over its indexed chunks. The points are plain text you can select and copy.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_QuickActions_Action3_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Translate</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_QuickActions_Action3_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Translates text you paste into the Input Text box (up to 4,000 characters; anything longer is cut off) into one of ten languages: Spanish, French, German, Chinese, Japanese, Korean, Portuguese, Italian, Russian or Arabic. It uses the active chat model and does not translate documents from the vault.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_QuickActions_Action4_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Find Duplicates</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_QuickActions_Action4_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Scan Exact Duplicates groups documents whose files have identical content (the same SHA-256 hash) and shows how much space the copies take. Scan Semantic Near-Duplicates compares stored embeddings to group documents that say nearly the same thing, with a confidence score. Both scans only report; nothing is deleted or merged.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_QuickActions_Action5_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Organize</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_QuickActions_Action5_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Analyze &amp; Suggest looks at up to 20 documents that are not in any Collection and suggests a Collection name and tags for each, with a confidence level and the reasoning. Suggestions are only shown; if you agree, create the Collection and add the tags yourself.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_QuickActions_Action6_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Recommended Actions</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_QuickActions_Action6_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>The Recommended For This Context panel at the top suggests up to four next steps from the selected document and the state of the app, such as finishing indexing, summarizing, triaging new Smart Inbox items, connecting a live source or scanning for near-duplicates. Each button runs the action or opens the page that handles it.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_QuickActions_Usage.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Open Quick Actions from the navigation rail or the Dashboard, pick a document in the list, choose a tab and click its button. Summarize and Key Points need an indexed document, Translate works on pasted text, and Duplicates and Organize look at the whole vault. Results appear on the page; there is no export, save-as-annotation or Continue in Chat.</value></data>

  <!-- UserGuide: Knowledge Vault (Row 7) -->
  <data name="UserGuide_KnowledgeVault_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Knowledge Vault</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_KnowledgeVault_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>The Knowledge Vault lists every document you have imported. For each file, Agent-X extracts the text, splits it into chunks, embeds them into the vector index (HNSW), adds the text to the keyword index (FTS5), and then asks the AI model for up to five tags. Semantic Search, Ask Your Files, Quick Actions, the Knowledge Graph and Workflows all read from the vault. The vault stores the extracted text and the path to the original file; the file itself is not copied and stays where it is.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_KnowledgeVault_Importing_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Importing Documents</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_KnowledgeVault_Importing_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Add documents to your Knowledge Vault in these ways:</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_KnowledgeVault_Import1.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Drag and drop - Drag files or folders onto the Knowledge Vault page. Folders are searched through all their subfolders, and only supported file types are taken. Before importing, Agent-X compares each file's content with the vault: exact copies of documents already there are listed as duplicates, and you choose Skip Duplicates or Import All.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_KnowledgeVault_Import2.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Import Files - Click Import Files and pick one or more files; the same duplicate check runs. The picker offers document, data and code formats only, so bring in images and audio by dragging them or with Import Folder. Web pages come in through the Web Import page or the browser extension.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_KnowledgeVault_Import3.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Folder import - Click Import Folder to import every supported file in a folder and its subfolders in one pass. To keep importing from a folder, add it under Watch Folders in Settings &gt; Knowledge Vault: while Auto-index watch folders is on, Agent-X imports the supported files already there, then new and changed ones while it runs.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_KnowledgeVault_Managing_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Managing Documents</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_KnowledgeVault_Managing_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Each row shows the file name, type, indexing status, the extracted or generated title, page, word and chunk counts, size, import date and tags. Search by file name, filter by file type, status, tag, Collection or import date, and sort by name, date, size or type. For each document you can:</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_KnowledgeVault_Manage1.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>View details - Select a row or click Detail to open the Document Preview panel with the file type and indexing status, the title, page, word and chunk counts, size, import date and tags. Open in Explorer shows the original file, and Workflow opens the Workflows page with the document.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_KnowledgeVault_Manage2.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Re-index - Re-index (on a row, in the preview panel, or for several selected documents) removes the document's chunks, embeddings and keyword entries and indexes it again from the original file, which must still be at the path it was imported from. Re-index after changing the embedding model or the chunk size or overlap in Settings.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_KnowledgeVault_Manage3.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Delete - Delete (on a row or for several selected documents) removes the document from the vault at once, without asking: its text, chunks, embeddings, keyword entries, tags and Collection memberships. The original file on disk is not touched. Turn on multi-select mode, or use Select All, to re-index or delete several documents together.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_KnowledgeVault_Advanced_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Advanced Features</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_KnowledgeVault_Advanced1.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Auto-Tagging — AI automatically generates semantic tags when documents are imported, categorizing content by topic, domain, and key concepts without manual effort.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_KnowledgeVault_Advanced2.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Duplicate Detection - Before an import, files whose content is identical to a document already in the vault are flagged so you can skip them. Near-duplicates are not checked at import; look for them with Quick Actions &gt; Duplicates, which only reports them.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_KnowledgeVault_Advanced3.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Batch Operations - Turn on multi-select mode to pick several documents, or use Select All, then Re-index or Delete them in one step. There is no bulk tagging; tags come from auto-tagging.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_KnowledgeVault_Advanced4.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Advanced Filtering — Filter documents by collection, date range, and sort by name, date, size, or file type to quickly locate specific files in your vault.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_KnowledgeVault_Advanced5.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Document Preview Panel — A side panel showing document details, tags, chunks, and metadata at a glance without navigating away from the document list.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_KnowledgeVault_Advanced6.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>AI-Generated Titles - AI Title (Generate Title in the preview panel) asks the model for a short title based on the start of the document and shows it under the file name. The file name itself does not change.</value></data>

  <!-- UserGuide: Collections (Row 8) -->
  <data name="UserGuide_Collections_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Collections</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Collections_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Collections group documents from your Knowledge Vault. A document can belong to several Collections at once, and deleting a Collection never deletes its documents. Semantic Search and Ask Your Files can be limited to one Collection, the Knowledge Vault can filter by one, and the Knowledge Graph shows each Collection as a hub linked to its documents.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Collections_Working_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Working with Collections</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Collections_Working1.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Create - On the Collections page, type a name and an optional description, then click Create Collection. Quick Actions &gt; Organize can suggest Collections for documents that are in none, but it does not create them for you.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Collections_Working2.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Add documents - Select a Collection, click Add Documents and pick files (PDF, Word, text, Markdown, CSV, JSON or HTML). New files are imported into the vault and added; a file whose content is already in the vault adds the existing document instead of a copy. A summary then says how many files were added, were already in the Collection, or failed. Web Import can also put the pages it imports into a Collection.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Collections_Working3.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Scope retrieval - In Semantic Search and Ask Your Files, pick a Collection to search only its documents; in the Knowledge Vault, the Collection filter shows only its members. You can pick one Collection at a time; Collections cannot be combined with AND or OR.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Collections_Working4.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Manage - Rename a Collection, export it, or remove single documents from it. Deleting one Collection happens at once, without asking; select several in multi-select mode to delete them together after a confirmation. Either way the documents stay in the vault.</value></data>

  <!-- UserGuide: Knowledge Graph (Row 9) -->
  <data name="UserGuide_KnowledgeGraph_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Knowledge Graph</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_KnowledgeGraph_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>The Knowledge Graph draws your Knowledge Vault as a network. Documents, Collections and tags are nodes; a line joins each document to its Collections and tags, and joins two documents that share at least one Collection or tag. Open it from the navigation rail or with Ctrl+G to see which documents cluster together and which stand alone.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_KnowledgeGraph_Elements_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Graph Elements</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_KnowledgeGraph_Element1.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Document Nodes - Circles in the Document color of the legend, sized by chunk count, so longer documents are larger. Hover a node to see its name, type and number of connections; click it to open Node Details, which also shows the file type.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_KnowledgeGraph_Element2.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Collection Nodes - Fixed-size hubs in the Collection color, each linked to its member documents. Node Details shows how many documents the Collection holds.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_KnowledgeGraph_Element3.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Tag Nodes - Smaller nodes in the Tag color, one per tag, whether it was assigned automatically or by hand (Node Details says which). Documents that share a tag are linked, so the layout tends to pull them together.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_KnowledgeGraph_Element4.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Edges - Solid lines. A link to a Collection or tag takes that node's color; a link between two documents uses a neutral tone and gets thicker the more Collections and tags they share. There are no similarity edges: the graph shows Collections and tags, not how alike two documents' contents are.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_KnowledgeGraph_Interaction_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Interaction</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_KnowledgeGraph_Interaction1.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Select and Highlight - Click a node to select it and open Node Details (type, name, subtitle, connection count and position). Clicking a Collection or tag also highlights its cluster, that node and everything linked to it, and dims the rest; click it again, click empty space, or use the clear button next to Cluster Active to reset. Nodes do not open documents, and there is no right-click menu.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_KnowledgeGraph_Interaction2.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Zoom, Filter and Search - Zoom with the mouse wheel or the zoom buttons (0.25x to 4x), and go back with Reset Zoom; the view cannot be panned. The SHOW check boxes at the bottom hide or show documents, Collections and tags. Typing in the search box highlights the nodes whose name or subtitle contains the text and shows how many matched.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_KnowledgeGraph_Interaction3.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Layout - Positions come from a force-directed simulation of 100 steps that runs when the graph is built: linked nodes pull together while all nodes push apart. The layout then stays fixed; Refresh rebuilds the graph from the current vault. There is one layout, with no physics controls or navigator panel, and leaving the page cancels a build that is still running.</value></data>

  <!-- UserGuide: Weekly Digest (Row 10) -->
  <data name="UserGuide_WeeklyDigest_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Weekly Digest</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_WeeklyDigest_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>The Weekly Digest is a statistical report on the last seven days in Agent-X, computed from your vault, conversations and search history. It counts what you imported, discussed and searched, compares the week with the seven days before it, and keeps each report so you can look back. No AI model writes it: every figure comes straight from the database.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_WeeklyDigest_Features_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Digest Features</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_WeeklyDigest_Feature1.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Activity Totals - Each report shows the week's new documents, new conversations, searches, tokens used in chat messages, and the storage the new documents added.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_WeeklyDigest_Feature2.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Top Lists with Trends - Up to five of the most repeated searches, the Collections that gained the most documents, and the most imported file types, each marked as new, up, down or flat against the previous seven days.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_WeeklyDigest_Feature3.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>On-Demand Reports - Click Generate New Digest on the Weekly Digest page to build a report covering the last seven days. Every report is kept in the digest history, so you can reopen earlier weeks. Digests are not produced on a schedule, and the seven-day window is fixed.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_WeeklyDigest_Feature4.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Conversation Highlights - The three most active conversations of the week, with their message and token counts. A report lives only on this page: there is no export, sharing or email delivery.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_WeeklyDigest_Feature5.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Report History - Earlier reports are listed with their date range and document and search counts; select one to reopen it. The digest does not flag unused documents, belief conflicts or knowledge gaps.</value></data>

  <!-- UserGuide: Semantic Search (Row 11) -->
  <data name="UserGuide_SemanticSearch_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Semantic Search</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_SemanticSearch_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Semantic Search matches the meaning of your query, not just its words. Ask "how does our login flow handle expired sessions?" and Agent-X finds passages about token refresh, session timeouts and re-authentication, even if they never use the words "expired" or "login". Hybrid mode adds FTS5 keyword search and merges both result lists with Reciprocal Rank Fusion, so exact terms and meaning count in one ranking.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_SemanticSearch_HowItWorks_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>How Vector Search Works</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_SemanticSearch_HowItWorks_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>When a document is indexed it is split into overlapping chunks, by default 512 tokens with a 50-token overlap (both set in Settings &gt; Knowledge Vault). Each chunk becomes a vector from the embedding model in use, for example 384 dimensions for all-minilm or 768 for nomic-embed-text. Vaults with fewer than about 10,000 embedded chunks are searched with an exact scan, larger ones through an HNSW index. At query time your question is embedded with the same model and compared by cosine similarity, and the best matches come back with a relevance score, the source document and an excerpt.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_SemanticSearch_Using_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Using Semantic Search</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_SemanticSearch_Using1.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Natural-language queries - Type a question, a topic or a phrase: "how does the authentication system work?", "Q3 revenue figures by region", "passages where I argued against monorepos". Semantic mode matches meaning, not just keywords.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_SemanticSearch_Using2.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Relevance - Each result shows a relevance bar and percentage: cosine similarity in Semantic mode, the BM25 score relative to the best hit in Keyword mode, and the fused rank score in Hybrid mode. Sub-scores are not shown. MIN RELEVANCE under Advanced (30% by default) drops weaker matches.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_SemanticSearch_Using3.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Filters - Narrow results by Collection and by file type (PDF, DOCX, text, code, Markdown, calendar events or email). Under Advanced, set the minimum relevance, the maximum number of results (20 by default, up to 100) and an import date range (CREATED AFTER and CREATED BEFORE), and sort by relevance, date or file name. Save stores the current query with its mode, advanced settings and sort order under Saved Filters.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_SemanticSearch_Using4.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Results - Each result shows the file name and type, the relevance bar, an excerpt of the matching chunk, its page number when the format has pages, the chunk number and the document's Collections. Open shows the file in File Explorer, and Workflow opens the Workflows page with the result loaded as input.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_SemanticSearch_Using5.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Three search modes - Semantic (vector similarity, best for concepts), Keyword (FTS5 full-text, best for exact terms, names, IDs and code) and Hybrid (both, merged with Reciprocal Rank Fusion, k=60). Choose one under SEARCH MODE; the page opens in Semantic mode. Ask Your Files uses hybrid retrieval.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_SemanticSearch_Using6.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>History and cache - Searches are kept in a history that persists across sessions. The Search History list shows recent queries (click one to run it again), and Clear empties it. Results are cached for five minutes, and the cache is cleared whenever documents are indexed, re-indexed or deleted.</value></data>

  <!-- UserGuide: Model Manager (Row 12) -->
  <data name="UserGuide_ModelManager_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Model Manager</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_ModelManager_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>The Model Manager lists the models of the active AI provider and lets you download, remove and switch between them without the command line. With Ollama it works on the Ollama models on your machine; with the built-in provider it works on the model files (.gguf) in its models folder; for OpenAI and Anthropic it only lists the models. The provider itself, the Ollama address and the embedding model are chosen in Settings.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_ModelManager_Capabilities_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Capabilities</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_ModelManager_Capability1.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>View installed models - Each model is listed with its name, family, parameter count, quantization, context length, size on disk, when it was last modified and a short digest, and the active model is marked ACTIVE. The header shows how many models there are and their total size.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_ModelManager_Capability2.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Model actions - Set Active makes a model the one the active provider uses everywhere, the same as choosing it in AI Chat, and remembers it after a restart. Copy model name puts the name on the clipboard. There is no detail view or benchmark.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_ModelManager_Capability3.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Pull new models - Type a model name, or click one of the suggestions (llama3.2, mistral, phi3, qwen2.5, gemma2, deepseek-r1), and click Pull Model. A progress bar shows the downloaded size and percentage, and Cancel stops the download. Browse Models opens the Ollama library in your browser. With the built-in provider, only the two models in its catalog (Llama 3.2 3B and 1B, by their .gguf file names) can be downloaded.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_ModelManager_Capability4.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Delete models - Reclaim disk by removing models you no longer use. Delete acts on one model at a time and takes effect immediately: there is no confirmation prompt and no undo, so pull the model again if you remove one by mistake.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_ModelManager_Capability5.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Defaults - Set Active is the only model choice made here. The provider, the Ollama address and the Embedding Model are set in Settings, and the Model Manager has no per-task assignments or hardware compatibility scores; the Hardware Advisor suggests models that fit your machine.</value></data>

  <!-- UserGuide: Hardware Advisor (Row 13) -->
  <data name="UserGuide_HardwareAdvisor_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Hardware Advisor</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_HardwareAdvisor_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>The Hardware Advisor reads your computer's GPU, processor, memory and NPU and suggests Ollama models that fit. It scans when you open the page, and Re-scan runs it again after a hardware change. Its suggestions depend on one number: the GPU's video memory, or the available system RAM when no GPU memory is reported. The advisor runs no benchmarks.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_HardwareAdvisor_Detects_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>What It Detects</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_HardwareAdvisor_Detect1.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>GPU detection - The graphics card's name and video memory come from Windows (WMI and the display driver's registry entry), and the card gets a tier by its memory: No dedicated GPU, Entry, Mainstream, Performance, Enthusiast or Professional. The advisor does not check CUDA, ROCm or oneAPI support.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_HardwareAdvisor_Detect2.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Processor and memory - The processor's name, cores and logical cores, total and available system RAM, and whether an NPU is present. When Windows does not report some of these, a notice says that some hardware details are unavailable.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_HardwareAdvisor_Detect3.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Recommended models - Under 4 GB: small models such as phi3:mini and qwen2.5:0.5b; 4 to 8 GB: llama3.2:3b and mistral:7b; 8 to 16 GB: 7B and 8B models such as llama3.1:8b and deepseek-r1:8b; 16 GB and more: large models such as qwen2.5:32b and llama3.1:70b-q4_0. Each tier also lists code models and one or two embedding models (all-minilm, nomic-embed-text, mxbai-embed-large), grouped as Chat, Code and Embedding.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_HardwareAdvisor_Detect4.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Install and advice - Models you already have are marked Installed, and Install downloads the others through the active provider, which works with Ollama (the built-in provider only downloads its own .gguf models). A short advisory explains what the detected GPU or CPU means for speed and which model sizes to expect. There are no quantization, context-length or GPU-offload settings here.</value></data>

  <!-- UserGuide: Settings (Row 14) -->
  <data name="UserGuide_Settings_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Settings</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Settings_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Settings is your full configuration surface. Every preference is persisted to a local JSON file, encrypted-at-rest where it contains secrets. Backups do not include settings. The page is organized into four collapsible groups — AI Provider, Inference, Knowledge Vault, and Cost Tracking — plus deep links to specialized surfaces like Sync, Encryption, Workspace Profiles, and Feature Flags.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Settings_Group1_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>AI Provider</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Settings_Group1_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Configure every model provider: Ollama endpoint URL (default http://localhost:11434, override for remote Ollama hosts); default chat and embedding models; OpenAI API key, organization, and active model (GPT-4o, GPT-4o-mini, o1, o3); Anthropic API key and active Claude model (Sonnet 4, Opus, Haiku); per-provider request timeouts, retry policies, and concurrent-request caps. Keys are stored DPAPI-encrypted on disk.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Settings_Group2_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Inference</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Settings_Group2_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Tune generation parameters: temperature (0.0 deterministic → 1.0 creative), top_p, top_k, frequency/presence penalties, max_tokens per response, default context window size, and stop sequences. Per-conversation overrides exist in Chat — these are the system-wide defaults for new conversations.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Settings_Group3_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Knowledge Vault</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Settings_Group3_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Control indexing behavior: chunk size (default 512 tokens), chunk overlap (default 50 tokens), and how many chunks are retrieved per query (top-K, default 5). Watch Folders lists the folders Agent-X imports from on its own: add one with Add Folder, with or without its subfolders, or remove it. The Auto-index watch folders switch turns importing from them on or off when you save.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Settings_Group4_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Cost Tracking</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Settings_Group4_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>For cloud providers, track per-message token usage and dollar cost against the live OpenAI and Anthropic rate cards. Drill into per-session, daily, weekly, and lifetime spend; break down by model, conversation, and feature (Chat, RAG, Quick Action, Workflow). Set monthly soft and hard budget caps that pause cloud calls automatically when exceeded.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Settings_Shortcut.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Press Ctrl+, from anywhere in the app to jump to Settings. Use the search bar at the top of Settings to find any specific option in seconds.</value></data>

  <!-- UserGuide: Keyboard Shortcuts (Row 15) -->
  <data name="UserGuide_KeyboardShortcuts_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Keyboard Shortcuts</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_KeyboardShortcuts_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Agent-X can be driven from the keyboard. The Command Palette (Ctrl+K) opens any page and a few actions, Jump To (Ctrl+P) opens a page, document or conversation, and the shortcuts below work anywhere in the app. F1 opens the Cheatsheet, which also lists the shortcuts of the page you are on.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_KeyboardShortcuts_Table_Shortcut.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>SHORTCUT</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_KeyboardShortcuts_Table_Action.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>ACTION</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_KeyboardShortcuts_Action1.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Open the Command Palette (pages, a few actions and the current page's shortcuts)</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_KeyboardShortcuts_Action2.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>New Conversation in AI Chat</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_KeyboardShortcuts_Action3.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Open Knowledge Vault</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_KeyboardShortcuts_Action4.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Open Semantic Search</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_KeyboardShortcuts_Action5.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Open Semantic Search (alternate)</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_KeyboardShortcuts_Action6.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Open Settings</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_KeyboardShortcuts_Action7.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Close Command Palette / dialog / dropdown</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_KeyboardShortcuts_Action8.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Show Keyboard Cheatsheet (live, searchable)</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_KeyboardShortcuts_Action9.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Jump To - open a page, document or conversation</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_KeyboardShortcuts_Action10.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Open Dashboard</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_KeyboardShortcuts_Action11.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Open Knowledge Graph</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_KeyboardShortcuts_Action12.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Open Workflows</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_KeyboardShortcuts_Action13.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Open Web Import</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_KeyboardShortcuts_Action14.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Open Analytics</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_KeyboardShortcuts_Action15.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Open Operations</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_KeyboardShortcuts_Action16.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Quick-jump to page 1-9 in the navigation order (Dashboard, Chat, AskFiles, Search, Vault, Collections, Workflows, Models, Settings)</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_KeyboardShortcuts_Footer.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Tip: Press F1 (or Ctrl+Shift+/) anywhere to open the Cheatsheet, which lists the global shortcuts plus the ones registered by the page you are on. Shortcuts are fixed in this release; they cannot be remapped.</value></data>

  <!-- UserGuide: Command Palette (Row 16) -->
  <data name="UserGuide_CommandPalette_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Command Palette</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_CommandPalette_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>The Command Palette is the keyboard route to every page in Agent-X, plus a few actions and the shortcuts that belong to the page you are on. Press Ctrl+K, type a few letters, press Enter.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_CommandPalette_HowToUse_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>How to Use</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_CommandPalette_Step1.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Press Ctrl+K (or Ctrl+Shift+P) anywhere in the app to open the palette over the current page; you can start typing right away.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_CommandPalette_Step2.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Type to fuzzy-match. The letters you type must appear in a command's name in that order but need not be adjacent, so "knv" finds Knowledge Vault and "tgt" finds Toggle Theme. Results keep the navigation rail's groups (Intelligence, Knowledge, Triage, System, Support), followed by Actions and, when the current page registers shortcuts, On This Page.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_CommandPalette_Step3.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Use Up and Down (or Tab and Shift+Tab) to move through results, Enter to run the selected command, and Esc or a click outside the palette to dismiss it.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_CommandPalette_Coverage.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>The palette lists every page on the navigation rail under the same names, icons and group placards, with the key chord beside any page that has one. It adds three actions (New Conversation, Import Files, Toggle Theme) and the shortcuts registered by the page you are on. A page added to the rail appears in the palette automatically. Documents and conversations are opened through Jump To (Ctrl+P), not the palette.</value></data>

  <!-- UserGuide: Supported File Formats (Row 17) -->
  <data name="UserGuide_FileFormats_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Supported File Formats</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_FileFormats_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Agent-X keeps only the text of the formats below; layout, formatting and pictures inside documents are dropped. Every file is split into chunks the same way (by paragraphs, then sentences, then words, up to the chunk size set in Settings), with PDFs split page by page, code in somewhat smaller chunks and tables in larger ones. Audio files are transcribed (see Audio Transcription), and .url and .webloc shortcuts import the page they point to. There are no parser or OCR settings.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_FileFormats_Table_Format.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>FORMAT</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_FileFormats_Table_Extension.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>EXTENSION</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_FileFormats_Table_Extraction.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>EXTRACTION METHOD</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_FileFormats_PDF.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>PDF</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_FileFormats_PDF_Extraction.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Text is read from the PDF's text layer page by page, so search results can show page numbers, and the title comes from the file's properties. Scanned PDFs without a text layer, encrypted or damaged files and fonts whose encoding cannot be decoded fail to import, with a message saying why; there is no OCR for PDFs.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_FileFormats_Word.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Word Document</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_FileFormats_Word_Extraction.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>The text of every paragraph, including those in tables, in document order; the first heading can become the title, and the author and other document properties are kept. Formatting and comments are ignored, and legacy .doc files are not supported.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_FileFormats_Text.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Plain Text</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_FileFormats_Text_Extraction.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Read as is: UTF-8 or UTF-16 when the file has a byte-order mark or is valid UTF-8, otherwise the Windows default code page. The same reader takes .csv, .log, .json and .xml files (and configuration files such as .yaml, .toml and .ini) as plain text, without parsing their structure.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_FileFormats_Markdown.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Markdown</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_FileFormats_Markdown_Extraction.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>.md, .mdx and .markdown files are turned into plain text with the Markdown syntax removed; YAML front matter is dropped, and the first level-1 heading becomes the title. Chunks follow paragraphs, not headings.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_FileFormats_Code.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Code Files</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_FileFormats_Code_Extraction.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Source files are read as text with their language recorded, and the first class, function, module or namespace name becomes the title. Chunks follow blank lines and sentences as for other text, only somewhat smaller; there is no syntax-aware splitting.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_FileFormats_Images.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Images</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_FileFormats_Images_Extraction.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Text in .png, .jpg, .jpeg, .bmp and .tiff images is read with the OCR built into Windows, in the languages of your Windows profile. An image without text is kept but has nothing to search, and if no OCR language is installed the import fails with a message. Images are not sent to a vision model.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_FileFormats_CodeList.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Code files: .cs, .js, .ts, .py, .java, .cpp, .c, .h, .go, .rs, .swift, .kt, .rb, .php, .html, .htm, .css, .scss, .sql, .sh, .yaml, .yml, .toml, .ini, .cfg and .xaml. Also supported: .pdf, .docx, .txt, .csv, .log, .json, .xml, .md, .mdx, .markdown, images (.png, .jpg, .jpeg, .bmp, .tiff), audio (.mp3, .wav, .m4a, .flac, .ogg, .webm; see Audio Transcription), and .url and .webloc web shortcuts. Excel, PowerPoint, EPUB, RTF and legacy .doc files are not supported.</value></data>

  <!-- UserGuide: Troubleshooting (Row 18) -->
  <data name="UserGuide_Troubleshooting_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Troubleshooting</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Troubleshooting_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Common problems and their fixes. The Operations page (Ctrl+Shift+O) lists recently imported documents with any indexing error and a Retry Index button, the Smart Inbox backlog, and sync and workflow status. The log files in %LOCALAPPDATA%\AgentX\Logs (one per day, kept for seven days) record errors in detail.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Troubleshooting_Issue1_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>"Ollama not detected"</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Troubleshooting_Issue1_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Ollama is needed only when it is the active provider. Check that it is running: in a terminal, 'ollama list' answers when the server is up, and 'ollama serve' reports that the address is in use when it is already running. Then check the Ollama endpoint under AI Providers in Settings (http://localhost:11434 by default) and click Test Connection. For Ollama on another computer, set OLLAMA_HOST there and allow its port through the firewall.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Troubleshooting_Issue2_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Slow responses</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Troubleshooting_Issue2_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Use a smaller model: the Hardware Advisor suggests sizes for your GPU memory or RAM, and a quantized variant such as Q4_K_M needs much less memory than FP16. For short questions, lower Context Window or Max Tokens under Inference in Settings, and close other programs that use the GPU. The built-in model runs on the CPU unless you raise localGpuLayers in settings.json while Agent-X is closed (there is no GPU setting in the app); Ollama uses a supported GPU on its own.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Troubleshooting_Issue3_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Documents not indexing</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Troubleshooting_Issue3_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>The Recent imported documents list on the Operations page shows why a document failed. Common causes: the format is not supported (see Supported File Formats) or the file has no readable text, such as a scanned PDF; no embedding model is available (with the default Embedding Model setting, the built-in model must be installed or Ollama must be running with all-minilm pulled); or the original file was moved or deleted, so it cannot be read again. Fix the cause, then use Retry Index in Operations or Re-index in the Knowledge Vault; there is no bulk re-index in Settings.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Troubleshooting_Issue4_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>High memory usage</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Troubleshooting_Issue4_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Use a smaller or more strongly quantized model (see the Hardware Advisor), lower Context Window under Inference in Settings, and close other memory-heavy programs. Web Import starts a headless browser for pages that need JavaScript, which uses extra memory while it runs. There are no settings for an in-memory chunk limit, for turning off that browser or for a disk-based vector index.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Troubleshooting_Issue5_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Search returns no results</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Troubleshooting_Issue5_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Check in the Knowledge Vault that the documents are indexed. Then try Hybrid or Keyword mode, which find exact terms that Semantic mode can miss, or describe what you want in a full sentence for Semantic mode. Clear the Collection scope and other filters, and lower MIN RELEVANCE under Advanced on the Search page (30% by default). If you changed the embedding model, re-index the documents.</value></data>

  <!-- UserGuide: Privacy & Security (Row 19) -->
  <data name="UserGuide_Privacy_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Privacy &amp; Security</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Privacy_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Agent-X keeps your data on your computer unless you choose a service that needs the network. The built-in model is the default provider, a cloud provider needs an API key and your choice in Settings, and the Dashboard's privacy line lists every service that is switched on to send data off the machine.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Privacy_Point1.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Stored locally - Documents' extracted text, chunks and embeddings, conversations, memories, beliefs, insights and the voice profile are kept in a SQLite database (agentx.db) under %LOCALAPPDATA%\AgentX. Settings are in settings.json in the same folder, with API keys encrypted by Windows (DPAPI). Database encryption (SQLCipher, AES-256) is off until you turn it on in Settings; its key is tied to your Windows account and cannot be recovered by anyone else.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Privacy_Point2.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Local by default - The built-in model runs on your computer and is the default provider. OpenAI or Anthropic are used only after you add a key and choose one as the active provider in Settings; the provider applies to the whole app, not to single conversations. Research Mode, the calendar and email connectors, model routing and sync each have their own switch, and Web Import only fetches what you ask it to.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Privacy_Point3.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>No telemetry - Agent-X sends no usage analytics, crash reports or logs of your AI calls anywhere. It connects to the network only for what you set up: the active AI provider (Ollama at its endpoint, or OpenAI or Anthropic), Research Mode web search, Web Import, the calendar and email connectors, and downloading the built-in model when you ask for it. The local API for the browser extension and the Android app listens only on this computer (port 9846) and needs its token. There is no network audit page.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Privacy_Point4.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Your data, your control - Agent-X does not upload your data or use it for training. Back up the database on the Backup &amp; Restore page. Deleting a document removes it from the vault at once (the original file stays on disk), and deleting a conversation asks first. There is no command that wipes all data; to start over, close Agent-X and remove the AgentX folder under %LOCALAPPDATA% after backing up anything you want to keep.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Privacy_Point5.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Cloud providers - With OpenAI or Anthropic active, your prompts and the conversation context go to that provider, and the Dashboard's privacy line says so. Each answer is saved with the name of the model that wrote it (exports can include it with Include model info), and the Cost Tracking card in Settings estimates the cost of OpenAI and Anthropic calls made since Agent-X started. How a provider stores or uses your data is governed by its own terms.</value></data>

  <!-- UserGuide: Getting Help (Row 20) -->
  <data name="UserGuide_GettingHelp_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Getting Help</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_GettingHelp_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>If this guide does not answer your question, check Troubleshooting and the Cheatsheet (F1) first. For anything else, email support.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_GettingHelp_Email_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Email Support</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_GettingHelp_Email_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Bug reports, feature requests and questions. Include your Agent-X version (shown at the bottom of the Settings page and in the status strip), the steps that lead to the problem, and the day's log file from %LOCALAPPDATA%\AgentX\Logs if something failed.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_GettingHelp_Docs_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Documentation</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_GettingHelp_Docs_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Longer guides come with the Agent-X source in its docs folder: the full user guide, a FAQ, troubleshooting steps, keyboard shortcuts, and references for the local API and plugin development.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_GettingHelp_Footer1.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Agent-X by Strategia</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_GettingHelp_Footer2.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Local-first AI for everyone</value></data>

  <!-- UserGuide: Temporal Identity (Row 11) -->
  <data name="UserGuide_TemporalIdentity_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Temporal Identity Coherence</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_TemporalIdentity_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Temporal Identity keeps a simple local record of how you think and write, built from what you do in the app: beliefs you state in chat messages, insight moments in AI replies, a basic voice profile, and how long you keep a conversation or a vault document open. It works with fixed word rules on English phrasing, not with an AI model. Past Self and the Dashboard read this record.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_TemporalIdentity_Tracking_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>What It Tracks</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_TemporalIdentity_Tracking1.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Beliefs - After each reply, your message is checked for sentences between 20 and 100 characters long that contain 'I think', 'I believe' or 'I feel' followed by 'that'. The rest of the sentence after 'that' (4 to 49 characters) becomes a belief's topic, up to five per message; the first sentence that mentions it becomes your stance, and a word-list score (good, agree or support against bad, disagree or wrong, for example) records how positive it is. Other phrasings are not tracked.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_TemporalIdentity_Tracking2.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Insights - After each reply, the conversation's AI replies are also scanned for marker words such as 'key insight', 'important' or 'realize', or an exclamation mark, and each matching reply is kept once as an insight. A highlight created through the annotation service becomes an insight too, but the app has no command for creating highlights yet. See Insight Harvesting.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_TemporalIdentity_Tracking3.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Voice - Each message you send updates a voice profile with two running averages: words per sentence, and a formality score that rises with 'therefore' or 'however' and falls with apostrophes, as in contractions. Past Self shows the profile, and Draft As Me uses it.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_TemporalIdentity_Tracking4.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Changes and reading time - When the positivity score of a known topic moves by more than 0.5, the change is recorded with the earlier and the new stance (see Conflict Detection). Agent-X also records how long a conversation stays open in AI Chat and a document stays open in the Knowledge Vault preview; a view under a second is skipped and one view counts for at most 30 minutes. No page shows these times yet.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_TemporalIdentity_BeliefEvolution_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Belief Evolution</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_TemporalIdentity_BeliefEvolution_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>There is no timeline view. On the Past Self page, type a topic exactly as it was recorded and click Show Belief Evolution: it says since when the belief has been tracked and whether it has changed, with the earlier stance and when it changed. Get Active Topics lists the topics recorded in the last 30 days in their exact wording.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_TemporalIdentity_Conflicts_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Conflict Detection</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_TemporalIdentity_Conflicts_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Each recorded change is a belief conflict. The Dashboard's Your Belief Evolution card lists up to five unacknowledged conflicts, largest change first, with the topic, the earlier and the current stance and the size of the change. Acknowledge a conflict to hide it for good. There are no resolution choices and no confidence setting, and acknowledging teaches the detector nothing.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_TemporalIdentity_Conflicts1.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Read a conflict as a prompt for reflection, not a verdict. The stances are sentences you wrote, and the change is measured only with the word-list score, so a reworded sentence can look like a change of mind while a real reversal may go unnoticed.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_TemporalIdentity_Conflicts2.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>A conflict card shows the topic, both stances and the size of the change (between 0.5 and 2). It does not link to the source conversations, show the time between the two stances or add any AI analysis, and it cannot be annotated.</value></data>

  <!-- UserGuide: Past Self (Row 12) -->
  <data name="UserGuide_PastSelf_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Past Self</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_PastSelf_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Past Self answers 'what did I think about this topic then?' from the record that Temporal Identity keeps, and it hosts Draft As Me. It looks topics up by their recorded wording; it does not read your conversations as free text or ask an AI model.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_PastSelf_Timeline_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Choosing a Time</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_PastSelf_Timeline_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>There is no timeline. Choose the moment to ask about with the Time period options: All time, Past week, Past month (selected by default), Past year or Custom date.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_PastSelf_Timeline1.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>All time asks for the stance you recorded first; the other options ask for the stance you held at that moment, so for a belief that changed after it you see the earlier stance. A belief that never changed shows the same stance whatever you choose.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_PastSelf_Timeline2.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Search Past Self only finds a topic typed exactly as it was recorded, with a capital first letter and the rest in lowercase. Get Active Topics lists up to 15 topics recorded in the last 30 days, so you can see the exact wording.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_PastSelf_Timeline3.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>There is no side-by-side comparison of two periods. To see whether a view changed, use Show Belief Evolution or the Dashboard's belief card.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_PastSelf_Querying_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Querying Past Self</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_PastSelf_Querying_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Type a topic and click Search Past Self. When it is found, the page shows your stance at the chosen time with a confidence bar, an excerpt from the message in which the belief was first recorded, up to three conversations and documents whose title or file name contains the topic and that date from within 30 days of that time, and your current stance if your view has changed since. Get Relevant Insights adds up to five insights whose text contains one of the words you typed.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_PastSelf_QueryExample_Label.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Past Self does not understand full questions such as:</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_PastSelf_UseCases_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Use Cases</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_PastSelf_UseCase1_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Intellectual Archaeology</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_PastSelf_UseCase1_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Look up a topic you have stated a view on to see the first stance you recorded (All time) and an excerpt of the message it came from.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_PastSelf_UseCase2_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Decision Review</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_PastSelf_UseCase2_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Pick the date of a past decision (Custom date) and search the topic to see the stance you held then, with conversations and documents on that topic from around that date.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_PastSelf_UseCase3_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Consistency Check</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_PastSelf_UseCase3_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Show Belief Evolution, or the Dashboard's belief card, tells you whether your stance on a topic has changed since it was first recorded.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_PastSelf_UseCase4_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Growth Tracking</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_PastSelf_UseCase4_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Get Active Topics shows what you have stated views on in the last 30 days, and Load Profile shows where your sentence length and formality have settled. There is no summary of how your thinking changed between two periods.</value></data>

  <!-- UserGuide: Generative Identity / Draft As Me (Row 13) -->
  <data name="UserGuide_GenerativeIdentity_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Generative Identity: Draft As Me</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_GenerativeIdentity_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Draft As Me, on the Past Self page, turns a short description of what you want to write, and an optional goal, into a starting draft of three or four sentences. The draft is assembled from fixed sentence templates picked by your voice profile's formality and sentence length. No AI model is called, and nothing else from your messages is imitated or quoted.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_GenerativeIdentity_HowItWorks_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>How It Works</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_GenerativeIdentity_Step1.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Each chat message you send updates the voice profile. Load Profile on the Past Self page shows the number of samples, the average sentence length and a style label (Casual, Balanced or Formal), or Not enough data before your first message.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_GenerativeIdentity_Step2.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Enter the context (what you are writing about) and, if you like, the goal, then click Generate Draft. The draft opens with a sentence chosen by your formality score, restates your context and goal, and ends with a stock closing sentence; before any voice sample exists, a neutral template is used.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_GenerativeIdentity_Step3.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Copy and Copy for Chat both put the draft on the clipboard, so paste it wherever you need it. There is no rating, no regeneration with adjusted settings and no way to insert the draft into a conversation directly.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_GenerativeIdentity_Voice_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Voice Analysis</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_GenerativeIdentity_Voice_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>The voice profile has two measures, both updated from your chat messages:</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_GenerativeIdentity_Voice1.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Sentence length - The average number of words per sentence, as a moving average in which each new message counts for 10%. An average of 10 words or fewer limits drafts to three sentences; otherwise they can have four.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_GenerativeIdentity_Voice2.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Formality - A score that rises when a message uses 'therefore' or 'however' and falls with each apostrophe, as in contractions; each new message counts for 5%. Vocabulary, signature phrases and punctuation habits are not analyzed.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_GenerativeIdentity_Voice3.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Style label - Below 0.3 the profile reads Casual, below 0.6 Balanced, and Formal above that. There is a single profile with no separate registers to choose from, and it cannot be edited or reset in the app.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_GenerativeIdentity_Access_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Accessing Draft As Me</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_GenerativeIdentity_Access_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Draft As Me is on the Past Self page only:</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_GenerativeIdentity_Access1.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Open Past Self, go to Draft as Me, fill in the context and the optional goal, and click Generate Draft. There are no length, tone or time-period options.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_GenerativeIdentity_Access2.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>AI Chat has no /draft or /refine commands, and Draft As Me cannot be started from a conversation.</value></data>

  <!-- UserGuide: Insight Harvesting (Row 14) -->
  <data name="UserGuide_InsightHarvesting_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Insight Harvesting</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_InsightHarvesting_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Insight Harvesting keeps short records of moments that looked significant. After each reply, Agent-X scans the conversation's AI replies for marker words and stores each matching reply once as an insight. Insights stay on your computer and appear only on the Past Self page, when you ask for relevant insights; they are not shown on the Dashboard, in the Weekly Digest or in search.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_InsightHarvesting_Automatic_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Automatic Detection</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_InsightHarvesting_Automatic_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Detection runs after every reply, over the AI replies in the conversation that were not captured yet, with fixed word lists:</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_InsightHarvesting_Automatic1.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Breakthrough words - A reply containing 'breakthrough', 'key insight', 'important', 'realize', 'discover', 'aha' or 'eureka' is captured, and these words add 0.2 to its base significance of 0.6.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_InsightHarvesting_Automatic2.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Excitement markers - An exclamation mark or 'amazing', 'incredible', 'fascinating' or 'interesting' also captures a reply and adds 0.1. Because these words are common, many ordinary replies are captured as well.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_InsightHarvesting_Automatic3.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Highlights - A highlight created through the annotation service becomes an insight with significance 0.7, its topic taken from its note or highlighted text. The app has no command for creating highlights yet, so in practice insights come from chat replies. Novel connections and synthesis are not detected.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_InsightHarvesting_Cards_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Stored Insights</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_InsightHarvesting_Cards_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Each insight stores the first 500 characters of the reply, a topic ('I think that ...' phrasing if the reply has it, otherwise General Insight), a significance score and the time it was captured. For example:</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_InsightHarvesting_CardExample_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>"The key insight is that productivity comes from doing less of what does not matter."</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_InsightHarvesting_CardExample_Content.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Captured from an AI reply because it says 'key insight'. Topic: General Insight. Significance: 0.8.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_InsightHarvesting_Managing_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Managing Insights</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_InsightHarvesting_Managing1.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Insights are listed only on the Past Self page: type a word and click Get Relevant Insights to see up to five insights with a significance above 0.5 whose text contains a word you typed, most significant first, each with its age and the reason it matched. There is no insight list on the Dashboard, no command-palette entry and no sorting or filtering.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_InsightHarvesting_Managing2.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>An insight does not link back to its conversation, and it cannot be copied with attribution, saved as an annotation or turned into a vault note.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_InsightHarvesting_Managing3.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Insights cannot be starred, dismissed, edited or deleted in the app, and no feedback tunes the detector; the word lists are fixed.</value></data>

  <!-- ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       USER GUIDE: GETTING STARTED TUTORIALS (NEW)
       ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <!-- UserGuide: First Chat Tutorial (Row 4) -->
  <data name="UserGuide_FirstChat_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Your First Chat</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_FirstChat_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>A 90-second hands-on tutorial that takes you from "fresh install" to "first useful conversation." By the end you will understand the chat surface, how to swap between local and cloud models, what conversation context means, and how to use the few keyboard shortcuts that make every subsequent chat 3× faster.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_FirstChat_Step1_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Step 1: Open AI Chat</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_FirstChat_Step1.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Click the AI Chat entry in the sidebar, press Ctrl+2 (the navigation order is Dashboard, Chat, AskFiles, Search, Vault…), or hit Ctrl+K and type "chat." The interface shows the conversation list on the left, the active conversation in the main pane, and the model selector + composer at the top and bottom respectively.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_FirstChat_Step2_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Step 2: Select a Model</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_FirstChat_Step2.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Use the model dropdown at the top of the conversation. It lists the models of the AI provider that is active in Settings: the built-in local model (the default), Ollama, OpenAI or Anthropic. To use a different provider, switch it in Settings first. With Ollama, llama3.2 is a good general-purpose local model. The dropdown shows model names only; it does not show context lengths or prices.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_FirstChat_Step3_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Step 3: Start Chatting</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_FirstChat_Step3.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Type a message in the composer at the bottom. Enter sends; Shift+Enter inserts a newline; Ctrl+Enter sends and immediately starts a new turn. Responses stream token-by-token, render full Markdown live (including code blocks with syntax highlighting), and end with three suggested follow-up questions. Press Esc at any point to stop generation; the partial response is preserved.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_FirstChat_Tips_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>First Chat Tips</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_FirstChat_Tip1.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Be specific. "Summarize this article" beats "summarize this." "Summarize this article in five bullets, focused on cost implications" beats both. Clear prompts produce dramatically better answers — especially on smaller local models.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_FirstChat_Tip2.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Press Ctrl+N to start a fresh conversation. Each conversation has its own context window, so a new conversation is the right move when you switch topics or want the model to forget prior framing. Pin important conversations to the top of the sidebar with the pin icon.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_FirstChat_Tip3.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Iterate with follow-ups instead of long opening prompts. Agent-X remembers everything within a conversation, so the second question can be very short ("now compare to last quarter") and still get the full context. This is faster than crafting one mega-prompt and produces better answers.</value></data>

  <!-- UserGuide: Your First Document (Row 5) -->
  <data name="UserGuide_FirstDocument_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Your First Document</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_FirstDocument_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Agent-X becomes substantially more useful the moment you connect it to your own documents. This tutorial walks you from "first import" to "first grounded answer with citations" in under three minutes. The same flow scales seamlessly from one file to thousands.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_FirstDocument_Step1_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Step 1: Import a File</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_FirstDocument_Step1.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Open Knowledge Vault (Ctrl+I), then drag a PDF, Word doc, Markdown, text, code, or web URL onto the window. Alternatively click Import to use the system file picker, paste a URL to ingest a web page, or drag a whole folder to recursively import everything inside it. The first import also kicks off auto-tagging and duplicate detection.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_FirstDocument_Step2_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Step 2: Wait for Indexing</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_FirstDocument_Step2.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Indexing extracts text via the format-specific parser, chunks the content into ~512-token semantic segments with overlap, runs each chunk through your embedding model, and writes vectors to the HNSW index plus FTS5 keyword index. Time scales with size: a typical PDF takes 5-30 seconds, a 1,000-page book around 2 minutes. Progress shows live in the Vault row and in Operations (Ctrl+Shift+O).</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_FirstDocument_Step3_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Step 3: Ask Questions</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_FirstDocument_Step3.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Open AI Chat, scope the conversation to the freshly imported document (or skip scoping to query the whole vault), and ask. Try "What are the main points?", "Summarize the key arguments and counter-arguments," or "What evidence does the author provide for their central claim?" Every answer renders with numbered citations linking back to the exact passages used.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_FirstDocument_Supported_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Supported File Types</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_FirstDocument_Supported.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Documents: PDF (with OCR fallback), DOCX, RTF, EPUB, HTML, Markdown, plain text, CSV/TSV, XLSX (sheet extraction), PPTX (slide extraction). Code: 30+ languages with syntax-aware chunking. Web: any URL via paste or the browser extension. Audio: MP3/M4A/WAV/FLAC via Audio Transcription. YouTube URLs trigger transcript ingestion. Images: PNG/JPG/JPEG via Tesseract OCR (and direct vision-model querying when the active model supports images).</value></data>

  <!-- ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       USER GUIDE: RESEARCH & WEB (NEW SECTIONS)
       ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <!-- UserGuide: Web Search (Row 13) -->
  <data name="UserGuide_WebSearch_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Web Search &amp; Research</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_WebSearch_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Research Mode adds live web search results to AI Chat answers. You choose one search provider in Settings (Brave Search, Serper or a SearXNG instance) and turn Research Mode on there, then switch it on in the chat with the globe button beside the message box. The text of each message you send becomes the search query, and the titles, addresses and snippets of the results go to the model with your question. Results are cached for the Cache Duration set in Settings.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_WebSearch_HowItWorks_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>How Web Search Works</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_WebSearch_Step1.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Set up a provider - In Settings, under Research Mode, choose the Search Provider: Brave Search or Serper (paste its API key) or SearXNG (enter your instance URL, for example http://localhost:8080). Only the selected provider is used, with no fallback to another one if it fails. Max Search Results (1 to 20, 10 by default) sets how many results an answer gets.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_WebSearch_Step2.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Search - With Enable Research Mode on in Settings and the globe button on in the chat, each message you send is searched as it is (the search gives up after 15 seconds). Pages are not opened or downloaded: only each result's title, address and snippet reach the model. If the search fails, times out or finds nothing, the answer is written without web results and a notice says so.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_WebSearch_Step3.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Answer - The model is asked to cite the results it uses as [1], [2] and so on. Under the answer, Web sources lists the results as numbered chips; click one to open the page in your browser (only http and https addresses open). The sources are saved with the message and come back when you reopen the conversation.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_WebSearch_Modes_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Using Research Mode</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_WebSearch_Mode1_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Two Switches</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_WebSearch_Mode1_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Research Mode needs both Enable Research Mode in Settings and the globe button beside the chat's message box. If the Settings switch is off or no provider is set up, the chat says once why no web search was made and answers without web results. Nothing detects on its own that a question needs current information.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_WebSearch_Mode2_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Sources in Exports</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_WebSearch_Mode2_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Because the sources are stored with each answer, a conversation export can include them: turn on Include citations in the export dialog to list each answer's sources under it, numbered as its [n] markers. There is no /search prefix and no separate Web button in the message box.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_WebSearch_Mode3_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Vault and Web Stay Separate</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_WebSearch_Mode3_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Research Mode works in AI Chat only, and AI Chat does not search your Knowledge Vault, so an answer never mixes vault and web sources and its citations carry no Vault or Web tag. To ask about your own documents, use Ask Your Files, which does not search the web.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_WebSearch_Tips_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Web Search Tips</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_WebSearch_Tip1.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Open a source - Click a numbered chip under an answer to read the page in your default browser. To keep a page, import it with Web Import; citations have no right-click import.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_WebSearch_Tip2.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Fresh results - The same query within the Cache Duration (60 minutes by default) reuses the cached results, so shorten the duration in Settings when a topic changes quickly. Wording such as 'as of today' does not start a search on its own; Research Mode has to be on.</value></data>

  <!-- UserGuide: Web Content Import (Row 14) -->
  <data name="UserGuide_WebImport_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Web Content Import</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_WebImport_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Beyond local files, Agent-X imports any web content into your Knowledge Vault — articles, blog posts, documentation pages, RSS/Atom feeds, sitemaps, even YouTube transcripts. Imported pages get the same treatment as local files: text extraction, chunking, embedding, FTS5 indexing, auto-tagging, and full availability to RAG, Search, Quick Actions, and Workflows.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_WebImport_Methods_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Import Methods</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_WebImport_Method1.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Paste URL directly — On the Web Import page (Ctrl+Shift+E) paste any URL, optionally tag it, and pick the destination Collection. Agent-X fetches, extracts, deduplicates against existing imports, and indexes — typically in 3-8 seconds for an article.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_WebImport_Method2.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Browser extension - Load the Agent-X Web Clipper in Chrome or Edge, paste the API token from Settings &gt; Connections into its popup, and clip the page you are reading: the full page, your selection, or a reader view. Clips go to the Smart Inbox for triage rather than straight into the vault. The extension talks to the desktop app through its local API on this computer, so nothing makes a cloud round-trip.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_WebImport_Method3.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Batch &amp; feed-based import - Paste several URLs, one per line, to import them in one run; point Agent-X at an RSS/Atom feed to import the items it lists right now; or import every page listed in a sitemap.xml. A feed is read once, when you import it: Agent-X does not re-check feeds on a schedule, so import the feed again to pick up new items.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_WebImport_Extraction_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Content Extraction</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_WebImport_Extraction_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Agent-X intelligently extracts the meaningful content from each page:</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_WebImport_Extraction1.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Strips navigation, sidebars, footers, ads, cookie banners, and chrome using a readability heuristic — only the main article body is kept. The supplementary helper preserves embedded image alt-text and figure captions where they add information.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_WebImport_Extraction2.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Preserves structure — heading hierarchy, lists, tables, blockquotes, code blocks, and inline links are all maintained so semantic chunking respects natural boundaries and citations resolve to specific sections.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_WebImport_Extraction3.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Captures rich metadata — author, publication date, canonical URL, og:image, language, and reading time. YouTube URLs trigger the YouTubeTranscriptHelper which pulls the auto-captions or published transcript as the document body.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_WebImport_JsRendering_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>JavaScript Rendering</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_WebImport_JsRendering_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>For SPA-style sites that load content dynamically (React/Vue/Svelte apps, paywalled sites with JS gates, infinite-scroll archives), enable JS rendering in Settings &gt; Web Import. Agent-X spins up a headless Playwright/Chromium instance, lets the page hydrate, optionally scrolls to trigger lazy loading, and captures the post-render DOM.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_WebImport_JsRendering_Note_Label.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Note</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_WebImport_JsRendering_Note.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>JS rendering is 5-15× slower than static fetch and uses ~200 MB of RAM per concurrent page. Enable it only for sites that need it. Per-domain overrides let you keep static fetch for fast sites and JS rendering for the few that require it.</value></data>

  <!-- UserGuide: Citations & Sources (Row 15) -->
  <data name="UserGuide_Citations_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Citations &amp; Sources</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Citations_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Transparency is non-negotiable in Agent-X. Every answer grounded in your documents or in live web sources ships with explicit, clickable citations — not hand-wavy "according to your files" gestures, but specific [1], [2], [3] markers tied to specific passages with verifiable provenance. A guard pass at render time validates that every citation number maps to a real retrieved chunk, so the model cannot invent citations even under hallucination pressure.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Citations_Types_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Citation Types</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Citations_Type1_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Document Citations</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Citations_Type1_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>For Knowledge Vault sources: file name, page number (PDF), heading path (Markdown/Word), or chunk index, plus relevance score and the exact retrieved passage. Click to open the document at that location with the matched span highlighted; right-click to add the source to a Collection or pin it for the conversation.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Citations_Type2_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Web Citations</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Citations_Type2_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>For live web sources: page title, canonical URL, source domain favicon, publication date when available, and relevance score. Click to open in your default browser; right-click to import the page into the Knowledge Vault as a permanent record so future RAG queries can ground in it without another web fetch.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Citations_Type3_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Multi-Source Answers</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Citations_Type3_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>When an answer fuses Knowledge Vault and Web sources, each citation is tagged with its provenance ("Vault" / "Web") and listed in a unified References panel grouped by source type. The model is instructed to attribute each claim to its specific source, so multi-source answers remain auditable rather than averaged into a single opaque blob.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Citations_Using_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Using Citations</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Citations_Using1.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Click any citation chip to expand the source preview, jump to the original location, or copy the cited passage with attribution to your clipboard for use in your own writing.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Citations_Using2.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Hover citation numbers inline in AI responses to surface a quick popup with the source title, score, and excerpt — verify a claim without breaking your reading flow.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Citations_Using3.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>In the Knowledge Vault, sort or filter by citation frequency to see which documents your conversations rely on most. High-frequency sources are obvious candidates for promotion to a pinned Collection or for deeper annotation.</value></data>

  <!-- UserGuide: Browser Extension (Row 16) -->
  <data name="UserGuide_BrowserExtension_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Browser Extension</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_BrowserExtension_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>The Agent-X browser extension, the Agent-X Web Clipper, is your in-browser bridge to the desktop app. Clip the page you are reading, just the text you selected, or a clean reader view, or clip every open tab at once, and each clip lands in the Smart Inbox for triage. The extension talks to the desktop app over its local API at http://localhost:9846, authenticated with a token you paste in once: no cloud, no proxy, no third party.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_BrowserExtension_Installation_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Installation</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_BrowserExtension_Step1.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Load the extension into Chrome or Edge (both Chromium-based browsers are supported): open the browser's extensions page, turn on Developer mode, choose Load unpacked, and select the browser-extension/dist folder of an Agent-X build. The extension is open-source and ships from the same repository as the desktop app.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_BrowserExtension_Step2.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>In Agent-X, open Settings &gt; Connections, make sure Enable Local API is on, and copy the API token. The local API listens only on this computer (http://localhost:9846), and a change to that switch takes effect on the next launch.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_BrowserExtension_Step3.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Pin the extension to your toolbar, open its popup, paste the token into the pairing field, and click Save. The header of the popup then shows the Agent-X version instead of Offline. There is no pairing code to confirm: the token is the pairing. It is stored in the browser and stays valid until you regenerate it in Settings &gt; Connections, which disconnects every client until you paste the new token.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_BrowserExtension_Features_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Extension Features</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_BrowserExtension_Feature1.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Clip modes - Full page captures the whole article, Selection captures only the text you highlighted, and Reader view keeps the main content without navigation and ads. Each clip carries the page title and URL along with the text.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_BrowserExtension_Feature2.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Clip all tabs - One button clips every tab in the current browser window, each as its own Smart Inbox item, which is handy at the end of a research session.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_BrowserExtension_Feature3.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Recent clips - The popup lists what you clipped recently, so you can confirm at a glance that a capture reached Agent-X. The extension has no context menu, keyboard shortcut, or side panel: everything starts from the popup.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_BrowserExtension_Feature4.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Triage before the vault - Clips do not go straight into the Knowledge Vault. They wait in the Smart Inbox, where you review each one and accept, defer, or reject it.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_BrowserExtension_NativeMessaging_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Local API Connection</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_BrowserExtension_NativeMessaging_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Each clip is an authenticated request to the Agent-X local API at http://localhost:9846, which answers only on this computer and blocks requests from web pages. Agent-X must be running with Enable Local API turned on: when it is not, the popup shows Offline and a clip fails instead of being queued for later, so clip again once the app is back. Nothing leaves your machine.</value></data>

  <!-- UserGuide: Mobile Companion (Row 17) -->
  <data name="UserGuide_MobileCompanion_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Mobile Companion App</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_MobileCompanion_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Agent-X Mobile is a companion app for Android, built with .NET MAUI, that reads from the desktop app's local API. It lets you browse your documents and conversations and run searches across your vault. It keeps no copy of the vault, and it cannot chat, capture, or change anything. The desktop listens on this computer only, so the phone reaches it through the Android emulator or a USB connection; Wi-Fi and remote connections are not supported yet.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_MobileCompanion_Pairing_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Connecting Your Device</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_MobileCompanion_Step1.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Install the Agent-X Mobile app on an Android phone or in the Android emulator. There is no iOS version yet.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_MobileCompanion_Step2.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Make the desktop reachable. The Android emulator reaches this computer at http://10.0.2.2:9846. For a phone, connect it over USB and run adb reverse tcp:9846 tcp:9846 on the computer, then use http://localhost:9846. Enable Local API must be on in the desktop's Settings &gt; Connections.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_MobileCompanion_Step3.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>In the app's Settings, enter that URL, paste the API token from the desktop's Settings &gt; Connections, tap Save, and tap Test Connection to confirm the desktop answers. There is no QR code or pairing code: the token is the pairing, and regenerating it on the desktop disconnects the phone until you paste the new one.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_MobileCompanion_Features_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Mobile Features</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_MobileCompanion_Feature1_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Conversation List</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_MobileCompanion_Feature1_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>See the conversations stored on the desktop with their title, model, message count, and last update. Reading messages and chatting are not available on the phone; open the conversation on the desktop.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_MobileCompanion_Feature2_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Knowledge Search</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_MobileCompanion_Feature2_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Search your Knowledge Vault from the phone. The query runs on the desktop, and each result shows the matching passage, its document, and a relevance score.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_MobileCompanion_Feature3_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Document List</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_MobileCompanion_Feature3_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Browse the documents in your vault with their size, import date, and indexing status. The app cannot add anything: voice notes, photos, and text notes cannot be captured from the phone.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_MobileCompanion_Feature4_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Live Connection Only</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_MobileCompanion_Feature4_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Nothing is cached for offline use. The app needs the desktop running and reachable, and it reports a connection error instead of showing stale data.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_MobileCompanion_Sync_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Connection &amp; Security</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_MobileCompanion_Sync_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>The phone reads everything from the desktop's local API on demand and stores only its own connection settings. The desktop listens on localhost only, and the app refuses plain HTTP to any other host, so traffic stays on the emulator bridge or the USB link. Every request carries the API token; regenerate it in Settings &gt; Connections to cut off every connected client at once.</value></data>

  <!-- ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       USER GUIDE: AUTOMATION (NEW SECTIONS)
       ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <!-- UserGuide: Workflows (Row 23) -->
  <data name="UserGuide_Workflows_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Workflows &amp; Automation</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Workflows_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Workflows are multi-step AI pipelines you compose from five building blocks — AI prompts, document lookups, text transforms, conditional branches, and output formatting — and run on demand. Chain them to turn repetitive work into a single click: summarize a document set, extract key points and reformat them as a report, or drive any custom text pipeline you would otherwise execute by hand. Build and run them visually in the Workflow Builder (Ctrl+Shift+W).</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Workflows_Creating_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Creating Workflows</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Workflows_CreateStep1.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Open Workflows from the navigation rail (or press Ctrl+Shift+W) and click New Workflow, or pick one of the built-in templates and click Use Template to start from it.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Workflows_CreateStep2.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Name the workflow and add steps. Each step is one of five types (AI Prompt, Document Lookup, Text Transform, Conditional Branch, Output Format), and each step's output feeds the next.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Workflows_CreateStep3.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Arrange the steps with the move up and move down buttons, remove any you do not need, and save. Steps transform text; no step sends notifications or messages.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Workflows_CreateStep4.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Run the workflow: enter the input text and click Run. The final output appears when the last step finishes, and you can save it to the Knowledge Vault; every run is also kept in the run history. Workflows run only when you start them: there are no schedules or event triggers.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Workflows_Triggers_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Running Workflows</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Workflows_Trigger1_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>On-Demand Runs</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Workflows_Trigger1_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Run any workflow from the Workflows page or the Workflow Builder whenever you need it. Each run takes your input text, feeds it through the step chain, and presents the final output.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Workflows_Trigger2_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Step Library</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Workflows_Trigger2_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Five step types compose every workflow: AI Prompt (call a model with a templated prompt), Document Lookup (pull content from your vault), Text Transform (case, extraction, dedupe, and more), Conditional Branch (if-then logic on step output), and Output Format (Markdown, HTML, or JSON).</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Workflows_Trigger3_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Run History</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Workflows_Trigger3_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Every run is recorded with per-step output, duration, and success status, so you can inspect exactly what each step produced and where a pipeline needs adjustment.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Workflows_Examples_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Example Workflows</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Workflows_Example1.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>"Meeting Minutes" — paste a raw transcript, extract decisions and action items with an AI Prompt step, and format the result as a Markdown summary you can file or share.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Workflows_Example2.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>"Research Digest" — look up documents from your Research collection with a Document Lookup step, have the model pull out key findings, and produce a structured briefing on demand.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Workflows_Example3.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>"Content Repurposer" — condense a long document with an AI Prompt step, then use a Conditional Branch: short outputs become social posts, longer ones become a formatted article draft.</value></data>

