  <!-- UserGuide: Advanced Queries (Row 31) -->
  <data name="UserGuide_AdvancedQueries_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Advanced Queries</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_AdvancedQueries_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Past simple questions, Agent-X supports a deep set of query techniques that materially improve precision, recall, and answer quality. The more deliberate your phrasing, the better your results — these techniques are the difference between "useful" answers and "remarkably good" ones, especially when your vault grows past a few hundred documents.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_AdvancedQueries_Techniques_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Query Techniques</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_AdvancedQueries_Technique1.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Collection &amp; tag scoping — Constrain retrieval with natural-language scope hints ("in the Competitors collection," "tagged 'pricing'") or use the scope chips above the composer. Combining a Collection with a tag commonly improves answer precision by 20-40% by eliminating off-topic chunks before retrieval. Hybrid retrieval also accepts boolean hints: "must contain 'ARR'" forces a keyword match alongside the semantic search.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_AdvancedQueries_Technique2.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Time-bounding &amp; recency — Phrase queries with explicit windows ("from the past week," "Q3 only," "after the Series B announcement"). Agent-X applies date filters to source metadata when present, and prompts the model to weight recent passages over older ones for evolving topics. Pair with a Collection filter for "what changed in the Pricing collection between Q2 and Q4."</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_AdvancedQueries_Technique3.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Comparative queries — Ask for explicit comparisons across documents or sources ("How do the three proposals differ on timeline?", "Compare our roadmap to what competitors announced this quarter"). Agent-X retrieves the top-K passages per source/document independently, then prompts the model to synthesize a comparative answer rather than blending sources into a single voice. Comparison view in the dedicated Comparison page surfaces side-by-side diffs.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_AdvancedQueries_Technique4.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Structured output - Ask for the shape you want: "answer as a Markdown table with columns Feature / Price / Pros / Cons," "give me a bullet list grouped by quarter," or "respond with a JSON object with these fields." Chat passes the request to the model as ordinary text and does not enforce a JSON format or schema with any provider, so check structured output before you paste it into a Workflow or another tool.</value></data>

  <!-- UserGuide: Performance Tuning (Row 32) -->
  <data name="UserGuide_PerformanceTuning_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Performance Tuning</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_PerformanceTuning_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Agent-X runs on a wide range of hardware. Out of the box it uses the built-in Llama 3.2 3B model on the CPU with an 8,192-token context window, 512-token chunks with 50 tokens of overlap, and five passages per Ask Your Files answer. The settings below are the ones that change speed and memory use; the Hardware Advisor suggests model sizes but does not change any setting.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_PerformanceTuning_Memory_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Memory Management</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_PerformanceTuning_Memory.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Embeddings are stored in the database. Up to about 10,000 chunks, searches scan them directly; above that, an HNSW index built in memory speeds searches up and takes roughly the size of the vectors (chunks x dimensions x 4 bytes) plus its graph links. A smaller embedding model keeps it small: all-minilm has 384 dimensions, the built-in model 3,072. Database encryption cannot be turned off once it is on. There are no settings for an in-memory chunk limit or a disk-based index.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_PerformanceTuning_Queries_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Query Optimization</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_PerformanceTuning_Queries.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Set Top-K Results in Settings &gt; Knowledge Vault (5 by default, 1 to 20) to choose how many passages Ask Your Files keeps for an answer. Lower values answer faster with fewer prompt tokens; higher values help recall on broad questions but cost more tokens and time. There is no MMR or cache setting: passages are spread across documents automatically, and search results are cached for five minutes.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_PerformanceTuning_Local_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Local Model Tuning</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_PerformanceTuning_Local.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>For Ollama models, a Q4_K_M quantization needs far less memory than Q8_0 or FP16 at a small cost in quality. Context Window under Inference in Settings (8,192 by default) is sent to Ollama and limits how much conversation context is assembled; lower it for short questions, raise it for long documents if memory allows. Ollama decides GPU offload on its own, and the built-in model uses the GPU only if localGpuLayers is raised in settings.json. For embeddings, all-minilm is much smaller and faster than mxbai-embed-large. Each reply in AI Chat shows its token count and speed, so you can compare models on your machine.</value></data>

  <!-- ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       USER GUIDE: EXTENDED FEATURES (NEW SECTIONS)
       ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <!-- UserGuide: Annotations & Highlights -->
  <data name="UserGuide_Annotations_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Annotations &amp; Highlights</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Annotations_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>The Annotations page lists highlights and notes attached to vault documents. In this version nothing in the app creates annotations: the vault preview has no highlighting, and chat, web search, the browser extension and Quick Actions cannot save a selection. The page shows annotations that already exist, for example ones that arrived through Collaborative Sync, and lets you search, edit, delete and export them.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Annotations_Creating_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Working with Annotations</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Annotations_Create1.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Search and filter - Type in the search box to find annotations whose highlighted text or note contains your words, or pick a color under FILTER BY COLOR; DISTRIBUTION shows how many annotations each color has.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Annotations_Create2.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Edit and delete - Edit an annotation to change its note and color. Delete removes it at once, without asking. Tags, Markdown in notes and links between annotations are not supported.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Annotations_Create3.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Export - Export as Markdown saves every annotation to a Markdown file you name.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Annotations_Surfacing_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Where Annotations Surface</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Annotations_Surface1.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Annotations page - The only place annotations appear. Each entry shows the document name, the highlighted text, the note and the color.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Annotations_Surface2.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Not in documents - The vault preview does not show highlights, and there is no export of annotated documents to PDF.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Annotations_Surface3.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Not in answers - Ask Your Files and AI Chat do not give annotated passages extra weight or point them out to the model.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Annotations_Surface4.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Insights only - An annotation created through the annotation service becomes an insight (see Insight Harvesting). Annotations are not part of the Weekly Digest or the Knowledge Graph.</value></data>

  <!-- UserGuide: Audio Transcription -->
  <data name="UserGuide_AudioTranscription_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Audio Transcription</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_AudioTranscription_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Agent-X transcribes speech on your own machine with a local Whisper model. Drop audio files (WAV, MP3, M4A, FLAC, OGG, WebM) on the Knowledge Vault page and each one is imported as a timestamped transcript that Semantic Search, Ask Your Files, Quick Actions and Workflows use like any other document. In AI Chat, the microphone button turns speech into text in the message box.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_AudioTranscription_HowItWorks_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>How Transcription Works</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_AudioTranscription_Step1.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Getting audio in - Drag audio files, or folders that contain them, onto the Knowledge Vault page, or use Import Folder; the Import Files picker lists document formats only. Each file is transcribed while it is imported, so a long recording takes a while to appear. In AI Chat, click the microphone to record and the stop button to finish, or right-click the microphone to pick an existing audio file; the text lands in the message box so you can check it before sending.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_AudioTranscription_Step2.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Conversion and transcription - Whisper reads only 16 kHz PCM WAV, so any other audio is first decoded, mixed down to mono and resampled into a temporary 16 kHz WAV file that is deleted afterwards. The app decodes WAV itself; MP3, M4A, FLAC and compressed WAV go through the Windows Media Foundation decoders, and OGG and WebM decode only when Windows has a codec installed for them. Transcription always uses the Whisper base model and runs on the CPU. The spoken language is detected automatically; there is no setting to force one.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_AudioTranscription_Step3.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>The transcript - An imported recording becomes a document whose text opens with a short header (file name, detected language, duration, model) followed by one line per Whisper segment, each prefixed with its time range, for example [00:01:05 --&gt; 00:01:12]. It is chunked and indexed like any other document, so the passages that search results and Ask Your Files sources show include those time ranges, and you can find the spot in your own audio player; Agent-X has no audio playback. Speaker diarization is not implemented, so transcripts carry no speaker labels.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_AudioTranscription_UseCases_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Use Cases</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_AudioTranscription_UseCase1.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Meeting recall - Import a meeting recording, then open Ask Your Files and ask "what did we decide about Q4 hiring?" The answer cites transcript passages, time ranges included, so you know where to listen in the original recording.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_AudioTranscription_UseCase2.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Podcast research - Import podcast episodes, group them in a Collection, and search or question them like any other documents: "Which episodes discuss X?" returns cited passages with the episode's file name and time range.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_AudioTranscription_UseCase3.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Dictation - Use the microphone in AI Chat to speak a question or a journal entry instead of typing it. After you send it, it is an ordinary chat message, so conversation memory, insight detection and the writing profile behind Draft as Me treat it like text you typed.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_AudioTranscription_Note_Label.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Note</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_AudioTranscription_Note.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Transcription needs the Whisper base model file, ggml-base.bin, in %LOCALAPPDATA%\AgentX\Models\Whisper. This release does not download it for you. Without it, AI Chat reports that a model is required, and imported audio is stored with a note in place of the transcript; after adding the model, re-index the document to transcribe it. Audio that Windows cannot decode is imported the same way, with a note that says so. No feature flag or setting turns transcription on or off.</value></data>

  <!-- UserGuide: Workspace Profiles -->
  <data name="UserGuide_WorkspaceProfiles_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Workspace Profiles</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_WorkspaceProfiles_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Workspace Profiles are saved presets. Each profile stores a name, a description, an Ollama model identifier, a comma-separated list of collection IDs, and free-form custom settings. Profiles are records only: selecting a profile or marking it as the default does not switch the model, change the collections in scope, or change any setting. All profiles share the same documents, conversations, and settings.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_WorkspaceProfiles_Creating_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Creating Profiles</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_WorkspaceProfiles_Create1.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Open Workspace Profiles from the sidebar, enter a name and an optional description in the left panel, and click Create Profile. The new profile opens in the editor.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_WorkspaceProfiles_Create2.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Enter the details you want to keep with the profile: an Ollama model identifier (for example llama3.2), collection IDs separated by commas, and any custom settings text. Click Save Profile to store them.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_WorkspaceProfiles_Create3.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Set as Default marks one profile as your default and removes the mark from the others. Turning the Default Profile switch off and saving removes the mark. Duplicate copies a profile, and Delete Profile removes only the profile. Agent-X does not load any profile at startup.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_WorkspaceProfiles_UseCases_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Use Cases</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_WorkspaceProfiles_UseCase1.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Model notes: record which Ollama model suits each project, then choose that model yourself in Settings when you switch projects.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_WorkspaceProfiles_UseCase2.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Project references: keep the collection IDs and notes that belong to a client or project in one place, so they are at hand when you pick that work up again.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_WorkspaceProfiles_UseCase3.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Variations: duplicate a profile to record a variation without changing the original.</value></data>

  <!-- UserGuide: Plugins -->
  <data name="UserGuide_Plugins_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Plugins &amp; Extensibility</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Plugins_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Plugins add capabilities to Agent-X. A plugin is a .agentx-plugin package (a ZIP archive with a manifest.json at its root; a plain .zip works too) that you install and manage on the Plugin Manager page. Today the app integrates two kinds: document processors, which teach the Knowledge Vault new file formats, and data connectors, which push items into the Smart Inbox. Plugins run inside Agent-X with your Windows account's rights and are not sandboxed, so install only plugins you trust. The built-in Calendar and Email connectors have their own pages and are not managed here.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Plugins_Manager_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Plugin Manager</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Plugins_Manager1.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Install - Click Install Plugin (or the + button) and choose a .agentx-plugin or .zip file. Agent-X checks the manifest, refuses a plugin that needs a newer version of the app (minAppVersion) or a dependency that is not installed, extracts the package to %LOCALAPPDATA%\AgentX\Plugins\&lt;plugin id&gt;, and records the plugin as disabled; nothing is loaded yet. Permissions listed in the manifest are informational only: nothing asks you to approve them and nothing enforces them.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Plugins_Manager2.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Enable - Turn a plugin on with its switch. Agent-X loads it into its own isolated assembly context together with the private dependencies in its folder, then runs its initialization and activation, allowing 30 seconds for each; a plugin whose declared dependencies are not enabled is refused. Enabled plugins start again with Agent-X, dependencies first, and one that fails to start is switched off rather than shown as running. The details panel shows the plugin's description, install path, dates and README; there is no per-plugin settings panel.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Plugins_Manager3.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Disable and uninstall - Switching a plugin off deactivates and unloads it; its files stay installed. Uninstall (the trash button, or Uninstall Plugin under Danger Zone) deletes the plugin's folder, including any data it kept there, and its record straight away, with no confirmation prompt; uninstalling several selected plugins asks first. If Windows still holds some of its files, Plugin Manager names the folder to delete after restarting Agent-X.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Plugins_Capabilities_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>What Plugins Can Do</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Plugins_Capability1.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Document processors - A plugin can read file formats that no built-in processor handles. While it is enabled, the Knowledge Vault offers it every file the built-in processors do not claim, so a plugin can add formats but cannot change how PDF, Word, text, Markdown, code, image, audio or web files are read. Files in the new format can be dragged onto the Knowledge Vault or picked up by Import Folder; the Import Files picker does not list them.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Plugins_Capability2.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Data connectors - A plugin can push external items, such as messages or feed entries, into the Smart Inbox through the inbox service Agent-X hands it; each new item is also imported into the Knowledge Vault as a searchable document. That inbox service is the only app service a plugin is given: the app does not hand plugins your stored Google or Microsoft sign-in tokens.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Plugins_Capability3.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Other plugin types - A manifest can also label a plugin as an AI provider, Quick Action, Workflow step or theme, and Plugin Manager lists it, but Agent-X does not call such plugins yet: they add no models, actions, workflow steps or themes.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Plugins_Capability4.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Isolation, not a sandbox - Each plugin loads into its own collectible assembly context, which keeps its dependencies apart from the app's and lets it be unloaded when you disable it. That separates code, not permissions: a plugin can reach your files and the network like any program you run.</value></data>

  <!-- UserGuide: Calendar & Email Integrations -->
  <data name="UserGuide_Integrations_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Calendar &amp; Email Integrations</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Integrations_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Connect Microsoft Outlook or Google (Gmail and Google Calendar) and Agent-X brings your calendar events and emails in alongside your documents: each synced event or message is added to the Smart Inbox and imported into the Knowledge Vault as a searchable document. These are the only supported providers; there is no CalDAV, IMAP, or Exchange Web Services connector. The connectors only read: Agent-X never sends, replies to, or changes anything in your account.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Integrations_Calendar_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Calendar Integration</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Integrations_Calendar1.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Connect Outlook (OAuth via Microsoft Graph) or Google Calendar (OAuth via Google). The calendar connector syncs events on a schedule (every 15 minutes by default) across a window of past and future days you choose, and you can run a manual sync at any time.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Integrations_Calendar2.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Ask natural-language questions across your calendar — "What's on my plate next week?", "Who am I meeting from Acme?", "Find every recurring meeting longer than 60 minutes," "Summarize Q3 meeting load by participant." Answers cite specific events with click-through to the source app.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Integrations_Calendar3.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Events do not trigger anything: there are no calendar-driven Workflows, automatic meeting briefs, or posts to other services. Once synced, events are documents like any other, so you can ask about them in Chat or Ask Your Files.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Integrations_Email_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Email Integration</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Integrations_Email1.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Connect Outlook (OAuth via Microsoft Graph) or Gmail (OAuth via Google); IMAP is not supported. Choose which folders to sync, how many messages to fetch per sync (50 by default), and how many days back to go (30 by default). Mail syncs every 10 minutes by default, and each message becomes a searchable document.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Integrations_Email2.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Query your email history — "Find every email about the Smith contract," "Summarize my conversation with Jane this quarter," "What deadlines did anyone send me this month?" Email threads are coalesced so a 12-message thread retrieves as one coherent unit, not 12 fragments.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Integrations_Email3.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Email does not trigger automation: there are no email-driven Workflows, and Agent-X does not draft, queue, or send replies. As messages sync, AI can sort them into categories (on by default), and that is the extent of the processing.</value></data>

  <!-- UserGuide: Backup & Restore -->
  <data name="UserGuide_BackupRestore_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Backup &amp; Restore</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_BackupRestore_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Backup &amp; Restore saves your Agent-X database, and optionally the pages saved by Web Import, to a single .agentxbak file that you can protect with a password. Restore replaces the current database with a backup's contents. Settings, API keys, logs, downloaded models, and plugins are not part of a backup.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_BackupRestore_Backup_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Creating Backups</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_BackupRestore_Backup1.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Manual backup: on the Backup &amp; Restore page, choose a Destination Folder and click Create Backup. To protect the file, turn on Encrypt backup (AES-256) and enter a password. The password is needed to restore and cannot be recovered.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_BackupRestore_Backup2.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Scheduled backups: off by default. Under Scheduled Backups on this page, turn on Back up automatically, set the interval in hours, how many scheduled backups to keep, the folder, and an optional password, then select Save Schedule. The schedule applies at once, and a backup that is due runs about five minutes later. Only the oldest scheduled backups beyond the limit are deleted; manual backups are kept.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_BackupRestore_Backup3.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>What's included: the Agent-X database (document text and chunks, embeddings, conversations, workflows, collections, annotations, and history) and, with Include indexed documents on, the pages saved by Web Import. Files imported from other folders are not copied. If database encryption is on, the database inside the backup stays encrypted with this installation's key, so only this installation, on the same Windows account, can restore it.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_BackupRestore_Restore_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Restoring from a Backup</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_BackupRestore_Restore1.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Choose a backup file under Restore from Backup and click Restore. If the backup is encrypted, Agent-X asks for its password. The backup and its database are checked before anything is changed.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_BackupRestore_Restore2.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Restore replaces the whole database with the backup's database and puts the backup's Web Import pages back, overwriting files with the same name; there is no merge or partial restore. If this fails, the previous database and files are put back. Settings and API keys are not changed.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_BackupRestore_Restore3.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Restart Agent-X when the restore completes; until then, open pages and cached search results still show the previous data. If Agent-X reports that the database is in use, another program, such as a second Agent-X window, has the database file open: close it and try again. Nothing is changed in that case.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_BackupRestore_Migration_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Cross-Version Migration</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_BackupRestore_Migration_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>A backup made by an earlier Agent-X version can be restored: the restored database is upgraded to the current version the next time Agent-X starts. Restoring a backup made by a newer version than the one installed is not supported.</value></data>


  <!-- UserGuide: Export & Data Portability (Row 34) -->
  <data name="UserGuide_Export_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Export &amp; Data Portability</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Export_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Your data is exportable in open formats at every level — single conversations, full conversation history, individual documents, the entire Knowledge Vault, your voice profile, your insights, your beliefs, your settings, your plugin manifests. There is no lock-in: if you ever leave Agent-X, you walk out with everything in formats you can read and load anywhere. Backup &amp; Restore (covered in its own section) is the round-trip story; this section is about one-way export.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Export_Conversations_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Conversation Export</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Export_Conversations.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>The Export conversation button at the top of AI Chat saves the open conversation as Markdown, HTML, PDF, JSON, plain text, CSV, Word (.docx), or PowerPoint (.pptx). Choose whether to include citations (the web sources of each Research Mode answer, listed with it), model info (the model that wrote each answer), metadata, and timestamps. JSON keeps the full structure, including the system prompt. A Markdown export can follow a template (Research Report, Executive Summary, or Annotated Bibliography), and Copy as Markdown puts the conversation on the clipboard instead of in a file. Export all conversations, at the bottom of the conversation list, saves every listed conversation in one Markdown file and tells you where it went.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Export_Documents_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Document &amp; Vault Export</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Export_Documents.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Export the entire Knowledge Vault as a ZIP containing the original files, the extracted text per document, the chunk index, and the vector embeddings (in a portable .vec format). The bundle is bit-for-bit reloadable on another Agent-X install — perfect for moving between machines, archiving a project, or handing a vault to a collaborator. You can also export a subset (single document, single Collection, date range) when you don't need the whole thing.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Export_Backup_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Automated Backups</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Export_Backup.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Scheduled backups are off by default and are set up under Scheduled Backups on the Backup &amp; Restore page. A backup holds the Agent-X database and, optionally, the pages saved by Web Import; settings, API keys, and plugins are not included. Protect a backup with a password before you store it in a cloud-synced folder.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Export_Restore_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Restoring Data</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Export_Restore.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Restore a backup on the Backup &amp; Restore page. Restore replaces the whole database; there is no merge or selective restore. A backup made by an earlier Agent-X version is upgraded the next time Agent-X starts after the restore.</value></data>


  <!-- Guide accuracy pass 2026-07: Comparison / Smart Inbox / Analytics / Operations / Collaborative Sync sections -->
  <data name="UserGuide_Comparison_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Document Comparison</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Comparison_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>The Comparison page generates a structured, AI-written comparative analysis of two or more documents from your Knowledge Vault. Select the documents, optionally set a focus topic to steer the analysis, and Agent-X retrieves each document's most relevant passages and synthesizes a side-by-side report.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Comparison_Feature1_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Structured Analysis</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Comparison_Feature1_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Reports break down into shared themes, key differences, and the unique points each document contributes — with an overall summary up top.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Comparison_Feature2_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Focus Topics</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Comparison_Feature2_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Add an optional focus topic ("pricing terms", "methodology") and the comparison concentrates on that dimension instead of a general overview.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Comparison_Feature3_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Markdown Export</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Comparison_Feature3_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Export any comparison report to Markdown for sharing, archiving, or pasting into your own documents.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Inbox_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Smart Inbox</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Inbox_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>The Smart Inbox is the triage queue for content entering your knowledge base. Captures from the browser extension and plugins land here first, so you decide what is worth keeping - instead of letting everything pile into the vault unsorted.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Inbox_Feature1_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>AI Previews</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Inbox_Feature1_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Agent-X drafts a preview for each item — a suggested summary, target collection, and tags — so most triage decisions take one glance and one click.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Inbox_Feature2_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Accept, Defer, or Reject</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Inbox_Feature2_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Accept an item to import it into the vault under your chosen collection, defer it to decide later, or reject it to discard. Batch actions clear a full queue in seconds.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Inbox_Feature3_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Capture Sources</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Inbox_Feature3_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Web captures from the browser extension and content contributed by plugins all flow into one queue with a consistent triage workflow.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Analytics_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Analytics</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Analytics_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>The Analytics page shows how your knowledge base and AI usage evolve: usage statistics, performance metrics, and activity trends across conversations, documents, and tokens — all computed locally from your own data.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Analytics_Feature1_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Usage Overview</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Analytics_Feature1_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Headline counters for conversations, messages, documents, and token consumption keep the size and activity of your workspace visible at all times.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Analytics_Feature2_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Activity Trends</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Analytics_Feature2_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Daily charts of conversations and document imports reveal your working patterns and how your knowledge base grows over time.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Analytics_Feature3_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Indexing Health</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Analytics_Feature3_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>A live indexing-completion indicator shows how much of your vault is fully embedded and searchable.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Operations_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Operations</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Operations_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>The Operations page (Ctrl+Shift+O) is the health console for everything running behind the scenes: connector status, collaborative sync state, indexing progress, and recent background activity — in one live snapshot.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Operations_Feature1_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Status Snapshot</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Operations_Feature1_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>See at a glance whether calendar and email connectors, collaborative sync, and indexing are healthy, idle, or need attention.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Operations_Feature2_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Suggested Fixes</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Operations_Feature2_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>When something is off, Operations lists the highest-leverage remediation steps based on the current snapshot — connect a live source, enable sync, or finish configuration.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Operations_Feature3_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Live Monitoring</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Operations_Feature3_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>The snapshot refreshes as operations progress, so long-running work like bulk imports and re-indexing can be watched without leaving the page.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Sync_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Collaborative Sync</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Sync_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Collaborative Sync keeps multiple Agent-X installations in step using an encrypted, file-based transport: point every device at a shared folder — OneDrive, Google Drive, a NAS, or even a USB drive — and changes flow between them with no server and no cloud account required.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Sync_Feature1_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Shared-Folder Transport</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Sync_Feature1_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Any folder every machine can reach works as the sync channel. Agent-X writes encrypted change packages to it — the shared location never holds readable data.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Sync_Feature2_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Conflict Resolution</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Sync_Feature2_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Timestamp-based conflict resolution decides which change wins when the same item was edited on two devices — no manual merge steps required.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Sync_Feature3_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Auto-Sync and History</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Sync_Feature3_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Turn on Auto-Sync to exchange changes at a regular interval, or press Sync Now whenever you want. Sync History lists every run with its result, so a failed pass is easy to find.</value></data>

